  "query_preview": ".. \nThe main weather threat will continue to be flash flooding,\nespecially across southern and eastern Utah. A slow moving upper\nlow over northern California northwest Nevada with a broad ridge\nto our east is helping to direct monsoon moisture northward across\nUtah. While isolated to widely scattered showers and thunderstorms\nwill continue to develop across much of the area this afternoon,\nthe strongest storms with the best threat of flash flooding will\nbe across southern and eastern Utah where the deepest moisture \nand CAPE values to around 1000 J/kg will be in place this \nafternoon. Forcing will continue increase across southeastern Utah\nby later this afternoon into this evening as a shortwave embedded \nin the southerly flow evolves northward out of Arizona. \n\nAs this wave pushes into eastern Utah this evening, the moisture \nand forcing will spread in farther to the west into southwest \nUtah as well with the deep southerly moisture flow favoring storms\ndeveloping in upslope prone areas from the south, such as the \nPine Valley mountains (Forsyth Burn Scar), as well as across the \nGrand Staircase east to Boulder summit. In addition, the \nconvergence into the terrain around Castle County, Price, and \nover the Tavaputs will also favor continued thunderstorm \ndevelopment and training. Of concern in southwest Utah, including \nthe Pine Valleys and St George area, is that heavy rainfall will \ntimed overnight, mainly between 9 PM MDT and the early morning \nhours on Thursday. Meanwhile, across eastern Utah overnight, as \nthe main shortwave moves through an area of broad light to \nmoderate rainfall is expected, which will help to further saturate\nthe soils and add water into area waterways. This may become a \nfactor into Thursday for mainly Emery and Carbon Counties where \nthere will be the highest potential for thunderstorms developing \nlater Thursday afternoon through Thursday evening off of the \ncentral mountains and moving into an environment with 30-35 knots\nof shear and 500-1000J/kg of CAPE. Training stronger \nthunderstorms and even a supercell cannot be ruled out there into \nThursday evening, and when combined with any rain received today \nand tonight, this may more easily pose a flood risk. \n\nThe potential for flash flooding will continue across southern and\neastern Utah through Friday as the upper level low remains to our\nwest allowing the monsoon moisture to remain in place. Finally by\nSaturday, models are in good agreement that the through will\nfinally pass Utah brining northwesterly winds ushering in cool \ntemperatures and drier air. However, this drying out trend will be\nshort-lived as ensembles are in good agreement that a cutoff low \nwill park near Las Vegas, allowing for a return to southerly flow \nacross southern Utah which will bring moisture back northward \nagain allowing for daily shower and thunderstorms chances to \nreturn again next week.",
